alue":"This is Part 3 in a series on gender gaps in life expectancy, what causes them, and what we can do about it.\nThis article identifies the causes of death that contribute most to the gap, focusing on the United States.\nIn the next article we’ll consider all OECD countries and identify policies that can narrow the gap.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"SsVnOjT9Xe"}],"key":"OIVtAijrNR"}],"key":"hXFvMXpa5o"},{"type":"thematicBreak","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"JnjW8zcyUY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"dpSQqXaY5Q"},{"type":"link","url":"https://allendowney.substack.com/p/what-drives-the-life-expectancy-gender","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"previous article","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"xXU9IQabPr"}],"urlSource":"https://allendowney.substack.com/p/what-drives-the-life-expectancy-gender","key":"d94pAXQocq"},{"type":"text","value":", we built a model to estimate how each cause of death contributes to the life expectancy gap.\nWe identified leading factors in statistical terms: coefficients and feature importance.\nBut the most important factor in the model might not be the most important factor for a particular country.\nFor example, drug disorders have low importance overall because the gap is small in most OECD countries.\nBut in the United States and Canada, drug disorders are among the leading contributors to the life expectancy gap.\nSo now we’ll ask a different question: ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"OIvIeD35rY"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"for a given country","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"pkiO9vSYFP"}],"key":"EQXu5dt1X9"},{"type":"text","value":", which causes of death offer the largest opportunity to close the gap?","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"SWzN7K1k72"}],"key":"nC6246yknR"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Counterfactual","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"TcEJUq6fre"}],"identifier":"the-counterfactual","label":"The Counterfactual","html_id":"the-counterfactual","implicit":true,"key":"Lqc9cRqget"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As an indication of what’s possible, we’ll consider the smallest observed gap for each cause-specific death rate.\nThe following table shows the minimum gap, in deaths per 100,000 people, across all 37 countries and 24 years, and the country and year where it occurred.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"FtH7kT6h12"}],"key":"Ifk0zuQZ2y"},{"type":"include","file":"tables/gap_extremes_min_blog_le.html","literal":false,"lang":"html","children":[{"type":"table","identifier":"t_49565","label":"T_49565","children":[{"type":"tableRow","children":[{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"identifier":"t_49565_level0_col0","label":"T_49565_level0_col0","class":"col_heading level0 col0","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cause","key":"WCnDZcA8ev"}],"html_id":"t-49565-level0-col0","key":"YkH6ClBqGo"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"identifier":"t_49565_level0_col1","label":"T_49565_level0_col1","class":"col_heading level0 col1","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Minimum gap","key":"LVEO1iqtk3"}],"html_id":"t-49565-level0-col1","key":"KiXSuyijri"},{"type":"ta